From a9c618efc3453e8abd9e8d528639fa6441e37904 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: a <a@a>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 10:50:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] merge fix_stops_aliases.py + fix_routes_compound_fields.py
 into fix_compound_csv_fields.py

Full audit of data/scripts/: everything else (clean_data.py,
validate_clean.py, import_data.py, test_clean_data.py, requirements.txt,
the 3 dedup/return-leg override YAMLs, verify_stop_coordinates.py) is
required by the active pipeline or a distinct ongoing QA tool -- left
untouched.

fix_stops_aliases.py and fix_routes_compound_fields.py were the one
genuine merge candidate: both are one-off fixers for the same class of
bug (an unquoted comma in a raw CSV field shifting later columns), same
brute-force-merge-and-validate strategy, and the routes one's own
docstring already said 'same idea as fix_stops_aliases.py'. Dry-run
against the current data/raw/*.csv confirms both are no-ops on today's
data (0 rows need fixing) -- the corruption they target is already
cleaned up upstream, kept only for the next raw-data refresh.

Merged into fix_compound_csv_fields.py:  picks
the column layout/validators, one shared CLI/harness (dry run by
default, --apply to write, backup taken first -- previously
inconsistent: the stops fixer wrote in place unconditionally with no
backup or dry-run option, now both use the safer routes-fixer contract).

Verified behavioral parity before deleting the originals: built
synthetic corrupted rows for both profiles (extra unquoted comma in
stops.aliases, in routes.operator) and diffed old-script-output vs
new-script-output byte-for-byte -- identical, including the routes
profile's trailing-column padding (n_pad) and the unfixed-row reporting
path. One gap caught and fixed during that verification: the first
merge draft dropped the n_pad padding, would have produced rows shorter
than the header on write -- fixed and re-verified before finalizing.

Also flagged (not changed, needs a human decision): merge_stops.py now
duplicates clean_data.py's built-in dedup_stops()/remap_stop_ids() (same
stop_dedup_overrides.yaml, applied against a live DB instead of at the
CSV stage) -- documented in data/scripts/README.md as redundant for the
standard workflow but kept for patching an already-imported DB without a
full reimport.

Noted, unrelated, not fixed: data/scripts/test_clean_data.py has 2
pre-existing failures (dedup_routes() return-value unpacking mismatch)
confirmed present at the original e76298e commit, before any of this
streamlining work -- not introduced by this change, out of scope here.
